      "writtenOn": "2014-08-23T20:18:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Let me try to explain why this shouldn\u0027t be optional. \n\n\nThe idea of OSprofiler is that it should allow project team to add any amount of trace points (that is required) with any amount of information. Because it\u0027s only on demand it won\u0027t make any sense on performance of whole cloud. \n\n\nTo add new points in code you have 4 ways: \n\n   from osprofiler import profiler\n\n   def some_func():\n        profiler.start(\"point_name\", {\"any_key\": \"with_any_value\"})\n        # your code\n        profiler.stop({\"any_info_about_point\": \"in_this_dict\"})\n\n\n    @profiler.trace(\"point_name\",\n                    info\u003d{\"any_info_about_point\": \"in_this_dict\"},\n                    hide_args\u003dFalse)\n    def some_func2(*args, **kwargs):\n        # If you need to hide args in profile info, put hide_args\u003dTrue\n        pass\n\n    def some_func3():\n        with profiler.Trace(\"point_name\",\n                            info\u003d{\"any_key\": \"with_any_value\"}):\n            # some code here\n\n    @profiler.trace_cls(\"point_name\", info\u003d{}, hide_args\u003dFalse,\n                        trace_private\u003dFalse)\n    class TracedClass(object):\n\n        def traced_method(self):\n            pass\n\n        def _traced_only_if_trace_private_true(self):\n             pass\n\n\nIf OSprofiler will be optional, you will be able to use only first option: \nstart()/stop(). Which will produce huge mess in code. Take a look at next sample\n\n\n  def some_func(*args, **kwargs):\n\n     #code\n \n\n  def some_func(*args, **kwargs):\n   try:\n      if profiler:\n         profiler.start(\"point_name\", {\"function\": {\"name\": \"some_func\", args ....}}) \n      #code\n   finally:\n      if profiler:\n         profiler.stop()\n\ninstead of\n\n   @profile.trace(\"point_name\") \n   def some_func():\n      #code\n\n\n\nSo I really do think that OSprofiler is not consumable if it is optional requirements...\n \n\nThe second thing is that it\u0027s already in cinder and glance, and it\u0027s going to be in heat and neutron. So usually it will be deployed on controller (so usually keystone will have OSprofiler always)",
      "parentUuid": "1abeadc6_a2fe8436",
